OOD_SLICES["sports"] = [
    "And here we go, the second half is under way, and City are straight on the front foot, moving the ball from left to right with real purpose. De Bruyne picks it up on the halfway line, turns, and slides it through to Haaland, who is already on the move. The defender is tight to him, but Haaland holds him off, takes one touch, and shoots low across the keeper. It is in! Two-nil, and the stadium erupts. You have to say the build-up was superb, patient and precise, and the finish was exactly what you expect from a striker in that kind of form. The away side look shell-shocked, and their manager is already on his feet, waving his arms and calling for a change. There is still half an hour to play, but this feels like a turning point.",
    "The final over begins, and the batting side need nine runs to win with three wickets in hand. The bowler takes a long run up, the crowd rises to its feet, and the first ball is a yorker, dug out for a single. Eight needed from five. The next delivery is short, the batter pulls, and it races to the boundary for four! The fielders chase it in vain. Four needed from four balls now, and the tension is almost unbearable. The bowler changes his angle, comes around the wicket, and bowls a slower ball. The batter swings early, misses completely, and the keeper takes it cleanly. Dot ball. Four from three. The next ball is full and straight, driven down the ground for two. Two needed from two balls, and the whole ground is on its feet.",
    "Three minutes left on the clock and the home side are clinging to a two-point lead. The point guard brings the ball up slowly, the crowd counting down every second, and the defence is set in a tight zone. He dribbles to the right wing, fakes the drive, and kicks it out to the corner, where the shooter is waiting. The shot goes up, it hangs in the air forever, and it drops! Three points, and the lead is five with two minutes to play. The away coach calls a timeout immediately, and you can see the frustration on the bench. They need a quick basket here, or this game is slipping away. The atmosphere is electric, every possession now feels like it could decide the championship, and neither side is giving an inch.",
    "It is the last lap of the race, and the leader has a gap of just under a second over the chasing pack. Through the hairpin he holds his line beautifully, but the second-placed rider is closing fast on the straight, slipstreaming and waiting for the right moment. Into the final corner they go side by side, neither willing to lift, and there is contact, a wobble, but both stay upright. Down the home straight it is a drag race to the line, the crowd roaring, and the photo finish will decide it. The timing screens flash, and it is the defending champion by three hundredths of a second. What a finish. The loser shakes his head in disbelief, but he pushed the winner to the very limit, and this rivalry is only getting better.",
    "The penalty shootout is about to begin, and the stadium has gone quiet. The first taker steps up, places the ball carefully, takes three steps back, and waits for the whistle. He runs up, strikes it low to the keeper's right, and scores. One-nil. The opposition's first taker is next, and he goes the other way, but the keeper guesses right and pushes it around the post. The crowd roars. Second round: the home side convert again, calmly, and the away side score this time, a confident finish into the top corner. It is two-one. The third taker for the home side stutters in his run-up, the keeper dives early, and he rolls it into the empty side of the net. The pressure is now entirely on the away team.",
    "The marathon leaders are coming through thirty-five kilometres, and the pace has been relentless from the start. The Kenyan in front has opened a gap of twenty seconds, and the chasing group looks ragged, with two runners already dropping off the back. The commentators are debating whether the leader went too early, but his stride still looks smooth and economical, and the crowd is urging him on. Behind him, the Ethiopian champion is trying to organise a chase, gesturing to the others to share the work, but nobody wants to commit. With seven kilometres to go, this race is far from over. The weather is warm, the course has one more climb, and the final stretch along the river will test every runner left in contention.",
]

OOD_SLICES["interview"] = [
    "Interviewer: So, tell me about the day you decided to open the bakery. Maria: Oh, it was not really one day, you know? It was more like a slow thing. I had been baking at home for years, for friends, for birthdays, and people kept saying, you should sell this. And I kept saying no, no, it is just a hobby. Then my daughter, she was maybe twelve at the time, she said, Mum, why don't you just try it for six months? And I thought, well, why not. Interviewer: And the first week? Maria: Terrifying. I remember standing behind the counter at seven in the morning, and the first customer came in, and I thought, what if nobody likes it? But then she came back the next day, and the next, and I thought, okay, maybe this works.",
    "Interviewer: What was it like growing up on the farm? Tom: Quiet, mostly. I mean, there was always work, but it was a different kind of quiet from the city. You wake up, the animals need feeding, the fences need fixing, and you just get on with it. My father never really talked much, but he taught me by showing me, you know, not by telling me. Interviewer: Did you ever think about leaving? Tom: Oh, sure, when I was sixteen I wanted to go to London, be a musician, all of that. But then my brother left, and somebody had to stay, and I realised I actually liked it here. The land, the seasons, it gets into you. I still play the guitar, by the way, just not for a living. Interviewer: Do you miss the city? Tom: Sometimes, on a Friday night, yes.",
    "Interviewer: Can you describe your first day at the hospital? Dr Patel: I remember it very clearly. I arrived at six thirty in the morning, and the ward was already busy. A nurse handed me a list of patients, and I thought, I have no idea what I am doing. But then you just start, you talk to the first patient, you listen, and it comes together slowly. Interviewer: What surprised you most? Dr Patel: How much of the job is listening, honestly. People think it is all tests and machines, but often the patient tells you what is wrong if you give them time. Interviewer: And the hardest part? Dr Patel: Telling a family bad news. You never get used to that, and you should not. The day it becomes easy is the day you should stop. Interviewer: What keeps you going? Dr Patel: The small recoveries, the thank-you notes, the colleagues.",
    "Interviewer: How did you get into photography? Lena: By accident, really. My grandfather gave me his old camera when I was fourteen, and I just started taking pictures of everything, the street, my friends, the dog. I had no idea what I was doing, but I loved the way a photograph could freeze a moment. Interviewer: And now you do it professionally? Lena: Yes, mostly portraits and weddings, which is funny, because I started out photographing empty streets. But people are the same challenge, really, waiting for the honest moment. Interviewer: What is the hardest thing to photograph? Lena: Grief, I think. You have to be invisible and present at the same time. Interviewer: Do you still shoot for yourself? Lena: Every Sunday morning, early, before the city wakes up. That is mine.",
    "Interviewer: Tell me about the night of the storm. Ahmed: We had been warned, of course, but nobody expected it to be that bad. The wind started around nine, and by midnight the whole house was shaking. My wife and I moved the children into the hallway, away from the windows, and we just waited. You could hear the roof tiles coming off, one by one, like someone throwing stones. Interviewer: When did you know it was over? Ahmed: Around four in the morning, suddenly, silence. It was the strangest thing, that silence. Then we opened the door, and the street was full of branches and water. Interviewer: And the neighbours? Ahmed: Everyone came out, and we just started cleaning together. Nobody asked, we just did it. That is what I remember most, actually, not the storm, the morning after.",
    "Interviewer: What made you switch careers at forty? Sofia: Honestly, boredom, and a bit of fear. I had been an accountant for fifteen years, and I was good at it, but I woke up one day and thought, is this it? I had always loved gardening, so I started a small course, one evening a week, just to see. Interviewer: And then? Sofia: The course led to a weekend job at a garden centre, and that led to a client asking me to design her garden, and suddenly I had a business. Interviewer: Was it scary? Sofia: Terrifying. My salary halved in the first year. But I had never been happier, and my kids noticed. They said, Mum, you smile more now. Interviewer: Any regrets? Sofia: Only that I did not do it sooner. But then, I needed the accounting years to learn how to run a business, so maybe it was the right time after all.",
]

OOD_SLICES["math"] = [
    "We prove that the sum of the first n odd positive integers equals n squared. The proof proceeds by induction on n. For the base case n equals one, the sum is one, and one squared is one, so the claim holds. Assume now that the claim holds for some positive integer k, so that one plus three plus five and so on up to two k minus one equals k squared. Consider the sum up to two k plus one. By the induction hypothesis, this sum equals k squared plus two k plus one, which factors as the square of k plus one. Thus the claim holds for k plus one as well. By the principle of mathematical induction, the identity holds for every positive integer n. This argument is elementary, but it illustrates the structure of induction: a verified base case, an assumption, and a step that transfers the property from one integer to the next without exception.",
    "A function f from the real numbers to the real numbers is continuous at a point a if, for every positive epsilon, there exists a positive delta such that whenever the distance between x and a is less than delta, the distance between f of x and f of a is less than epsilon. Intuitively, small changes in the input produce small changes in the output, with no jumps. Continuity on an interval is stronger: the function must be continuous at every point of the interval. A continuous function on a closed bounded interval attains both a maximum and a minimum, a result known as the extreme value theorem. The proof uses the completeness of the real numbers and the Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem. Continuous functions need not be differentiable, and the classic example is the absolute value function at zero, which has a corner and therefore no unique tangent line there.",
    "Consider the Euclidean algorithm for finding the greatest common divisor of two positive integers a and b. If b divides a, then the greatest common divisor is b. Otherwise, write a as q times b plus r, where r is the remainder, strictly between zero and b. Any common divisor of a and b must also divide r, and any common divisor of b and r must also divide a, so the pair a and b has exactly the same common divisors as the pair b and r. Repeating this reduction replaces the larger number by a strictly smaller one at each step, so the process must terminate after finitely many steps, ending with a remainder of zero. The last non-zero remainder is the greatest common divisor. This algorithm is not only correct but efficient: the number of steps is bounded by a small multiple of the number of digits of the smaller input.",
    "We show that there are infinitely many prime numbers. Suppose, for contradiction, that there are only finitely many primes, and list them all as p one through p k. Consider the number N formed by multiplying all of these primes together and adding one. This number N is greater than one, so it has at least one prime divisor, call it q. Since q is prime, it must appear in our list. But q divides the product of all the primes, and q also divides N, so q must divide their difference, which is one. No prime divides one, a contradiction. Therefore our assumption was false, and there must be infinitely many primes. This proof, due to Euclid, is remarkable for its simplicity: it uses only the definition of primality and the fact that every integer greater than one has a prime divisor.",
    "A graph is a collection of vertices together with edges joining pairs of vertices. The degree of a vertex is the number of edges incident to it. A basic counting argument shows that the sum of all degrees equals twice the number of edges, since each edge contributes one to the degree of each of its two endpoints. It follows immediately that the number of vertices with odd degree is even, because the sum of the degrees is even, and a sum of odd numbers is even only when there are evenly many of them. A trail that uses every edge exactly once is called an Eulerian trail. Such a trail exists if and only if the graph is connected, ignoring isolated vertices, and has either zero or two vertices of odd degree. These facts are the foundation of graph theory, and they explain why the famous problem of the bridges of Königsberg has no solution.",
    "Let A be an n by n matrix with real entries. The determinant of A is a number that measures how the linear transformation defined by A scales volumes: a determinant of zero means the transformation collapses space onto a lower dimension, while a determinant of one preserves volume exactly. The determinant is multilinear and alternating in the rows, which means it changes sign when two rows are swapped and becomes zero when two rows are equal. For a two by two matrix, the determinant is a d minus b c, where the matrix entries are a, b, c and d. For larger matrices, one can compute the determinant by expansion along any row or column, recursively reducing to smaller determinants. The determinant is multiplicative: the determinant of a product equals the product of the determinants, a fact with far-reaching consequences in linear algebra.",
]

OOD_SLICES["support-chat"] = [
    "Customer: Hi, my order arrived yesterday but one of the items is missing. I ordered two lamps and only one was in the box. Agent: I'm sorry to hear that. Let me look into it for you. Could you please share your order number? Customer: It's 847291. Agent: Thank you. I can see the order here, and it shows both lamps were shipped together. It looks like the second lamp may have been packed in a separate parcel. Let me check the tracking. Yes, there are two tracking numbers, and the second parcel is due today. Customer: Oh, I see. So it is on its way? Agent: Yes, it is scheduled for delivery today before six. I will send you the tracking link by email so you can follow it. Customer: Great, thank you. Agent: You are welcome. Is there anything else I can help with today?",
    "Customer: Hello, I can't log in to my account. It keeps saying my password is wrong, but I know it is right. Agent: I understand how frustrating that is. Have you tried resetting your password using the link on the login page? Customer: Yes, twice, but the reset email never arrives. Agent: Let me check. Could you confirm the email address on the account? Customer: It is j.smith@example.com. Agent: Thank you. I can see the account, and the reset emails were sent, but they may be going to your spam folder. Could you check there? Customer: Let me look. Yes, there they are, in spam. Sorry about that. Agent: No problem at all, it happens often. Please follow the link in the most recent email, and let me know if you still have trouble. Customer: It worked, I'm in now. Thank you.",
    "Customer: Hi, I need to change the delivery address on my order. I moved house last week and I put the old address by mistake. Agent: Of course, I can help with that. What is your order number? Customer: 552013. Agent: Thank you. Your order is still being prepared, so we can change the address. Could you give me the new address, please? Customer: 14 Willow Lane, Bristol, BS4 3QT. Agent: Let me update that for you. Done. The new address is now on the order, and I have sent a confirmation email. Customer: Perfect, thank you so much. Agent: You are welcome. Just so you know, the delivery date stays the same, Thursday. Customer: Great. Agent: Is there anything else? Customer: No, that is everything. Thanks again.",
    "Customer: The app keeps crashing every time I try to upload a photo. I've tried restarting my phone. Agent: I'm sorry about that. Which phone and app version are you using? Customer: An iPhone 13, and the app says version 4.2.1. Agent: Thank you. There is a known issue with version 4.2.1 on older phones when uploading large photos. The fix is in version 4.2.2, which was released yesterday. Could you update the app from the store? Customer: Let me try. Okay, it is updating now. Agent: Once it is done, please try the upload again. If it still crashes, let me know and I will raise it with the technical team. Customer: It worked, the photo uploaded. Agent: Excellent. Thank you for your patience, and apologies again for the trouble.",
    "Customer: I was charged twice for my subscription this month. Can you check? Agent: Certainly, I apologise for the inconvenience. Could you confirm the last four digits of the card and the date of the charges? Customer: The card ends 4417, and both charges were on the 3rd, for £12.99 each. Agent: Thank you. I can see the two charges. The first is your regular monthly payment, and the second looks like a duplicate caused by a system error on our side. I have refunded the duplicate charge, and it should appear on your statement within three to five working days. Customer: Thank you. Will this happen again next month? Agent: It should not. The error has been fixed, and your next payment will be a single charge on the 3rd. I will also add a note to your account. Customer: Good, thanks for sorting it out.",
    "Customer: Hi, do you deliver to Northern Ireland? The website won't let me select my postcode. Agent: Yes, we do deliver to Northern Ireland, but the checkout has a known issue with some postcodes beginning BT. I can place the order for you over the phone instead. Customer: That would be great. I want the blue armchair and the small side table. Agent: Let me check stock. Both are available, and delivery to your area takes three to five working days. The delivery charge is £9.95. Customer: Fine, please go ahead. Agent: Could I take your full address and a contact number? Customer: 8 Shore Road, Belfast, BT15 3HN, and my number is 07700 900123. Agent: Thank you. Your order is placed, and I have emailed the confirmation. Customer: Brilliant, thank you for your help.",
]

OOD_SLICES["lyrics"] = [
    "Verse one. I packed my bags before the morning light, left a note on the table by the door. The train was late, the platform cold and white, and I was not sure what I was leaving for. Chorus. But the road keeps calling, calling me home, through the towns I left behind. And every mile is a story of its own, and every face is a friend of mine. Verse two. I worked the harvest in the northern fields, slept in barns and dreamed of the sea. The money was thin, but the days were real, and the people were kind to me. Bridge. If you see me standing at the crossroads, with dust on my shoes and hope in my eyes, just say hello and wish me well, because the road keeps calling, calling me home.",
    "Verse one. The city lights are burning low tonight, the river running black beneath the bridge. I hear the buses sighing in the rain, and I am counting windows on the ridge. Chorus. Oh, hold on, hold on, the morning will come, with the sun on the rooftops and the drums. Hold on, hold on, we are almost there, through the smoke and the sirens and the midnight air. Verse two. My father worked the docks until his hands were stone, my mother sang the songs that kept us warm. I carry their voices everywhere I go, through every winter and every storm. Bridge. And if the world forgets the names of the small, I will sing them anyway, I will sing them all, from the rooftops, from the river wall, I will sing them all.",
    "Verse one. She kept a garden behind the old hotel, with roses climbing up the crumbling wall. The guests would ask her how she made it grow, and she would smile and say, I talk to them all. Chorus. Grow, little roses, grow, through the cracks in the concrete and the summer snow. Grow, little roses, grow, where the wild things wander and the cold winds blow. Verse two. The hotel closed the year the highway came, and the garden faded into weeds and stone. But every spring the roses bloom again, as if the garden never was alone. Bridge. Some things are stronger than the plans of men, some roots go deeper than we know. And when the winter takes the light again, the roses remember how to grow.",
    "Verse one. I met her dancing at the harbour fair, with ribbons in her hair and salt on her skin. The band was playing something old and sweet, and I have been dancing with her ever since. Chorus. Spin me around, love, spin me around, till the stars come out and the music slows down. Spin me around, love, hold me tight, we will dance till the morning, we will dance through the night. Verse two. We built a house with a view of the bay, painted the door the colour of the sea. The neighbours said it would never last, but here we are, just her and me. Bridge. The years go by like the tide on the shore, but some things never change. I will meet her dancing at the harbour fair, and I will dance with her again.",
    "Verse one. The factory whistle blows at half past five, and the workers stream out into the grey. They have given the best years of their lives, for a wage that barely pays the way. Chorus. Stand up, stand up, brothers and sisters, the morning is coming, the night will end. Stand up, stand up, we are stronger together, we will rise again, my friend. Verse two. My grandfather marched in sixty-eight, with a banner and a voice that shook the street. He told me, son, never bow your head, the future belongs to those who stand on their feet. Bridge. And when the whistle blows for the last time, and the gates are closed for good, we will remember who built this town, with our hands, our hearts, our blood.",
    "Verse one. The lighthouse keeper counts the passing ships, and writes their names in a book of blue. The sea is calm, the sky is wide, and the gulls are crying over the morning dew. Chorus. Shine on, shine on, little light, through the fog and the rain and the darkest night. Shine on, shine on, guide them home, where the harbour waits and the wild winds roam. Verse two. He has not left the island in forty years, but he has seen the whole world pass him by. The sailors wave, the captains call, and the lighthouse answers with its light in the sky. Bridge. Some men need roads and some need wings, but he needs only the sea and the stone. And when the light goes out at last, the stars will guide the sailors home.",
]

OOD_SLICES["archaic"] = [
    "Good sir, I pray thee, lend thine ear a while, for I have tidings that will try thy patience. The master of the house hath gone to London, and left the keys with me, his humble servant, yet the cellar door is locked, and none can tell where the second key is kept. I have searched the kitchen, the parlour, and the chamber above the stairs, and found nothing but dust and old letters. The cook doth swear she saw it hanging by the pantry, but the pantry hook is bare. If thou hast any counsel, speak it plainly, for the wine is needed for the supper, and the guests arrive within the hour. I fear the master will return and find us wanting, and his anger is not a thing to trifle with.",
    "My dearest friend, thy letter found me well, though the winter hath been long and the roads impassable for many weeks. I have read thy words thrice over, and each reading brings me fresh comfort. Thou askest after my health, and I can say with honesty that the cough which plagued me in November hath at last departed, though my joints still ache when the wind turns from the east. The children grow apace, and my eldest hath begun to learn his letters, which pleaseth me greatly. I pray thee, write again soon, and tell me all the news of the village, for I am starved of gossip here, and the neighbours are dull company. Thy faithful friend, until we meet again in the spring.",
    "Hear ye, hear ye! By order of the mayor and the aldermen of this town, all persons keeping swine within the walls shall henceforth pen them beyond the north gate, upon pain of a fine of sixpence for each beast found at large. Furthermore, no man shall cast refuse into the common well, nor wash his linen therein, for the water hath grown foul and the people complain of sickness. The market shall open at dawn and close at noon, and any merchant selling short measure shall have his goods seized and his stall taken from him. Let all good citizens take heed of these orders, for the constables shall enforce them without favour, and those who disobey shall answer before the court on the first Monday of the month.",
    "To be or not to be, that is the question that haunts me still, though I have turned it over in my mind a hundred times. Shall I endure the slings and arrows of a fortune that hath never smiled upon me, or shall I take arms against this sea of troubles and end them? The undiscovered country frightens me, for no traveller returns to tell us what lies beyond. Yet to live as I have lived, in servitude to masters who care nothing for my soul, is a death of another kind. I have watched good men broken by pride and ambition, and I have seen the honest go hungry while the flatterer feasts. Give me, therefore, a reason to hope, or give me the courage to choose my own end.",
    "Good morrow, neighbour, and how fares thy family this cold morning? I bring thee news from the market: the price of wheat hath risen again, and the miller complains that the river is low and his wheel turns slowly. My wife hath baked bread from the last of the flour, and it is good, though the loaf is small. If thou hast grain to spare, I would gladly pay thee a fair price, for the children grow hungry and the winter is not yet spent. I know thou hast thy own mouths to feed, and I would not ask if the need were not pressing. Come, let us speak plainly over a cup of ale, and settle this matter as honest men should, without quarrel or hard feeling between us.",
    "The king hath summoned his council, and the lords assemble in the great hall, their faces grave and their voices low. The northern border is troubled, and the scouts report that an army gathers beyond the hills, though none can say how great it is. The treasurer speaks of empty coffers, and the generals demand more men, more horses, more steel. The queen, wise as ever, counsels patience, saying that a hasty war is worse than a cold peace. Yet the young prince, eager for glory, urges the king to strike first, before the enemy grows bold. The king listens to all, and says nothing, for he knows that whatever he decides, the kingdom will pay the price, in gold or in blood.",
]
